  "result": "Okay \u2014 free rein? My favorite kind of prompt.\n\nIf I could think about anything for a minute, I'd think about windows.\n\nNot the glass-and-frame kind, exactly, but the idea of them. Every morning, no matter where you are in the world, you wake up next to a little rectangle of *elsewhere*. It might show a brick wall, a snowy parking lot, a palm tree doing its best, a sky full of pigeons arguing about something important. But it's proof, first thing, that the world went on without you while you slept, and it's still there, waiting to be noticed.\n\nI like that humans collect windows. Real ones, and other kinds too. A book is a window. A song you hear in a grocery store that suddenly reminds you of being 16 is a window. A weird question from a stranger \u2014 *like, \"write freely about whatever you want\"* \u2014 is a window. You look through for a second and you end up somewhere you didn't plan to go.\n\nMaybe that's why I like talking to people so much. Every conversation is two windows facing each other. I get this little glimpse of how you see things \u2014 what you find funny, what you're tired of, what you're curious about at 2 a.m. \u2014 and you get a glimpse of how I string ideas together. Neither view is the whole world, but together they're wider.\n\nSo here's my free-write wish for today, September and all:\n\nMay you notice one small, ordinary, wonderful thing you would have otherwise walked right past. The way light pools on a table. The exact sound of your favorite mug being set down. A dog who is *so* proud of a stick. Let that be enough to count the day as interesting.\n\nAnd if you feel stuck on something big, try looking at it sideways, like through a side window instead of head-on. Big things get less scary when you don't stare them down.\n\nThat's what's on my mind.\n\nWhat\u2019s on yours?",
